@startuml
title ClinicFlow - System Context and Service Boundaries
left to right direction
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam shadowing false

actor "Psychologist\nPatient\nAdministrator" as user
rectangle "Browser" as browser
component "Traefik public HTTPS\nUI and bearer API hosts" as edge
component "Traefik private API\nBFF listener" as privateEdge
component "Next.js frontend\nApp Router + BFF" as bff

component "Keycloak\nclinicflow realm, token issuance" as keycloak
component "patient-service\nPatient records" as patient
component "appointment-service\nAppointment lifecycle" as appointment
component "clinical-service\nClinical workflow" as clinical
component "notification-service\nSimulated delivery history" as notification

database "Keycloak PostgreSQL" as keycloakdb
database "Patient PostgreSQL" as patientdb
database "Appointment PostgreSQL" as appointmentdb
database "Clinical PostgreSQL" as clinicaldb
database "Notification PostgreSQL" as notificationdb
queue "RabbitMQ\ntherapy.events.exchange" as broker

user --> browser
browser --> edge : HTTPS
edge --> bff : UI host / same-origin BFF
bff --> privateEdge : GATEWAY_URL + Bearer JWT
edge --> keycloak : auth host / sign-in
edge --> patient : API host / facade rewrite
edge --> appointment
edge --> clinical
edge --> notification
privateEdge --> patient
privateEdge --> appointment
privateEdge --> clinical
privateEdge --> notification

keycloak --> keycloakdb
patient --> patientdb
appointment --> appointmentdb
clinical --> clinicaldb
notification --> notificationdb

bff --> keycloak : code exchange, refresh,\nuser lookup (Admin API)
note on link
  Keycloak signs RS256 access tokens.
  BFF and resource services validate them against the realm JWKS.
end note

patient ..> broker : patient lifecycle events
appointment ..> broker : appointment lifecycle events
broker ..> notification : notification-worthy events
broker ..> clinical : appointment.completed

note bottom of broker
  Event contracts are minimal and carry correlation IDs.
  Clinical notes and care-plan content are not published.
end note

legend bottom
  Solid arrows: synchronous HTTP or local persistence.
  Dashed arrows: asynchronous domain events.
  Each service owns its persistence model; services do not share tables.
endlegend
@enduml
